# Godot's import cache and editor state. Regenerated on open; never committed.
.godot/

# `*.import` files ARE committed, deliberately, and this line used to ignore
# them. They carry the per-file import settings -- and for
# assets/terrain/heightfield_overview.png those settings decide whether the
# RGB bytes survive at all. Height is encoded IN those bytes (R high, G low),
# so a VRAM-compressed import would silently corrupt the terrain while every
# checksum still passed. Losing the settings to .gitignore means the next
# clone re-imports with whatever the defaults are that week.
#
# `src/terrain/heightfield.gd` also asserts the loaded format at runtime, so
# the guarantee does not rest on a file being present.

# `.uid` files are NOT ignored, deliberately. Godot 4.4+ emits one per script
# and they are meant to be version-controlled: they are what keeps a reference
# stable when a file moves. They appeared here the first time the project was
# opened under 4.7, so this note exists to stop someone reading them as stray
# editor output and ignoring them.

# Export artefacts.
/export/
*.pck
*.zip

# Large sim artefacts fetched rather than committed. `assets/*/README.md`
# says which tool produces each and how it is pinned; the bytes are not this
# repo's to carry until the LFS-vs-fetch call is made (roadmap Phase 5 (e)).
# The terrain export COMMITS. At 6.0 MB -- a 1 km overview plus manifests --
# it is the "small overview + manifests" half of appendix (e)'s split, and
# committing it is what lets a fresh clone build the viewer from the repo
# alone. What does NOT commit is the full-resolution tile pyramid, which the
# export declares and does not emit for exactly that reason.
/assets/terrain/tiles/
# The client fixture COMMITS, at 25 MB. It is the quantised DISPLAY form --
# band rows over the contract's declared bounds -- not the fixture itself: the
# full-precision payload is 76 MB and stays in the simulation repo, because
# three of its rows are float64 for values below float32's floor and no colour
# ramp can show those. Committing the display form is what lets a fresh clone
# run M2 from the repo alone; anything needing the precision goes upstream.

.DS_Store

# Blender writes a .blend1 backup beside every save. The source is the .blend;
# the backup is the previous save of the same file and belongs to nobody.
*.blend1

# Screenshots from tools/screenshot.sh. Regenerable, and a frame is large;
# quote the measurement in a commit message rather than committing the picture.
shots/
